/**

@page page_exagrype ExaGRyPE

@image html tutorials/exahype2/exagrype/exagrype_logo.png

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
\tableofcontents

ExaGRyPE is a numerical relativity application built based on the engine
ExaHyPE2, whose current version conduct simulation of vacuum and black hole spacetimes.
ExaGRyPE solves the Einstein field equations in the standard CCZ4 formulation under a 3+1 foliation,
and employs a block-structured Cartesian grid carrying a higher-order Finite Difference scheme with full 
support of adaptive mesh refinement (AMR). It facilitates massive parallelism combining message passing, 
domain decomposition and task parallelism, and it supports the injection of particles into the grid as 
static data probes or as moving tracers.

ExaGRyPE is actively under development as a part of the ExaHyPE projects and is evolving as a novel 
numerical relativity code within the computational astrophysics field. ExaGRyPE is also a member of the <a href="https://www.uknumericalrelativity.org/home">UKNR community</a>. Planned enhancements include optimizing
its performance, porting it to GPUs, and extending its capabilities to simulate more complex scenarios beyond
black hole spacetimes. These advancements will involve incorporating matter components via standard Euler fields
or Smoothed-Particle Hydrodynamics (SPH). Additionally, thanks to the high flexibility in dimensionality offered
by ExaHyPE2, future versions will enable full simulations of modified gravity theories in higher dimensions.

The release paper of ExaGRyPE is provided below for interested users. The code is completely open-sources and can 
be found in the Peano repositories. The @ref page_exahype2_tutorials "ExaHyPE 2 tutorials" include a tutorial series on how to install
and run the code on a cluster or your laptop. 


<div style="background-color: #ccf ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  As ExaHyPE's applications are built on top of ExaHyPE, we decided to embed 
  all application-specific content straight into ExaHyPE. Consequently, all
  data are scattered: The application-specific benchmarks are part of ExaHyPE's
  benchmark collection, the tutorials are a subset of the tutorials (which makes
  sense as they build on top of ExaHyPE and Peano), and so forth. However, 
  deep crosslinks are provided below.
</div>


# Physics Note
## Vacuum Spacetime

The ExaGRyPE code solves the Einstein equations in the Z4 formulation

@f$
    R_{ab}-\frac{1}{2} g_{ab}R+\nabla_{a} Z_{b}+\nabla_{b} Z_{a}-g_{ab}\nabla^c
    Z_c -\kappa_{1}[n_{a} Z_{b}+n_{b} Z_{a}+\kappa_{2} g_{ab} n_{c}
    Z^{c}]  =  8\pi T_{ab}
@f$

Noabely, we transfer it into a first order formulation following 

@f$
  \partial_t Q +\nabla_i F_i(Q) +B_i (Q) \nabla_i Q = S(Q).
@f$

Collecting all derived quantities from CCZ4 decomposition, the gauge quantities and 
helper variables yields an evolving system of over 24 variables

@f$
  Q(t) = 
  \left( \tilde{\gamma}_{ij}, \alpha, \beta^i, \phi, \tilde{A}_{ij},K, \Theta,
  \hat{\Gamma}^i, b^i \right)(t),
@f$

if the auxiliary varibles representing the first-order derivatives:

@f$
   A_{i}:=\partial_{i} \alpha, \ B_{k}^{i}:=\partial_{k} \beta^{i}, \ D_{k i
   j}:=\frac{1}{2} \partial_{k} \tilde{\gamma}_{i j}, \
   P_{i}:=\partial_{i} \phi
@f$

are calculated on-fly. In this case we have the second-order formulation of the code. 
Adding the auxiliary variables to the system and treating them as further
evolving variables make the code completely first-order, yielding a system of 58 independent 
variables with 

@f$
  Q(t) = 
  \left(
   \tilde{\gamma}_{ij}, \alpha, \beta^i, \phi, \tilde{A}_{ij},K, \Theta, \hat{\Gamma}^i, b^i, A_k, B^i_k,
    D_{kij}, P_k \right).
@f$

Both formulations are now available in the code for difference scenarios. Users are referred to Appendix B
of the paper below for more details of equations and formulation used in ExaGRyPE.

